This is mainly for Breakup anthems but you can mold it to fit what your song is about.

💃 1. Start With a Scene That Hits 
📝 Tip: Open your song with a vivid visual or detail that sets the tone immediately.
“Red dress on the bedroom floor” — cinematic, dramatic, unforgettable.

🔁 2. Play With Repetition But Add a Twist 
📝 Tip: Repetition = catchy. But add a clever twist to keep it from feeling stale.
“You're bad for me... but tonight I'm bad for you.” — Iconic.

💔➡️👑 3. Use Emotional Contrast (Pain vs Power) 
📝 Tip: Glow-up in the lyrics. Take us from heartbreak to hot boy/girl revenge in one track. (Breakup anthem tip)
“Had me beggin’ please” ➡️  “Got these boys all over me.”

📱 4. Inject Personality Into Every Line 
📝 Tip: Let your lyrics sound like you. Use slang, tea, or current lingo to make it feel real real. (Perfect for mainstream pop music makers) 
“Got your friends all in my DMs” — messy, modern, legendary!

🎣 5. Focus on Hooks Within Hooks 
📝 Tip: Drop lil mini-hooks throughout your song that could be IG captions, TikTok sounds, or viral tweets.
📸 “You were so bad for me / but it felt so good” — that’s a bar! Add onomateopia-worded Hooks like ooh-ooh or woah cause most popular songs do have catchy parts for longevity!

🎤 6. Let the Chorus Deliver the Final Punch 
📝 Tip: End your chorus with a line that hits like a plot twist. Make it the moment everyone screams at the club. Perfect for Breakup anthems.
("But tonight I'm bad for you ✨)

Hope this helps! 🤍🤍

